            <ArticleTitle>Antianginal effects of nitroglycerin during exercise-induced angina: hemodynamic and left ventricular function changes related to indexes of myocardial oxygen consumption.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>10H-14H</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In 14 patients during exercise, intravenous nitroglycerin improved anginal threshold and increased workload compared with control subjects. At similar workloads, the decreased left ventricular volumes suggested decreased myocardial oxygen consumption due to peripheral unloading. At maximal exercise with nitroglycerin (50 +/- 17 to 79 +/- 15 watts), rate-pressure product and end-diastolic volumes were higher with less ischemia, suggesting myocardial supply was improved by nitroglycerin.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
